.\" env.1 -- maux's env(1), in the V7 man(1) shape. An original
.\" page (this project's own text, not copied from a historic
.\" env(1)), so it carries no third-party notice -- MIT like the
.\" code, same posture as xargs.1. Rendered at build time to
.\" build/man/ENV.TXT and staged on the play image as
.\" /usr/man/ENV: `more /usr/man/ENV`.
.TH ENV 1 "September 14, 2026" maux
.SH NAME
env \- run a utility in a modified environment, or print it
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B env
[
.B \-i
] [
.IB name = value
] ... [
.I utility
[
.I argument
] ... ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Env
obtains the current environment, modifies it according to its
.B \-i
and
.I name=value
operands, and either prints the result (one
.I name=value
per line, if no
.I utility
was given) or runs
.I utility
with it.
.TP
.B \-i
Start from an empty environment rather than this process's own.
.TP
.IB name = value
Add
.I name
to the environment with the given
.IR value ,
or replace it if
.I name
is already there. Any number of these may precede
.IR utility ;
the first operand containing no
.B =
ends them and is taken as
.IR utility .
.PP
.I Utility
is resolved against
.B PATH
exactly as a shell resolves a bare command word (execvp), and
its
.IR argv [0]
is the word as typed, not the resolved path.
.I Env
exits with
.IR utility 's
own exit status.
.SH FILES
/usr/man/ENV  this page
.SH "SEE ALSO"
sh(1), xargs(1), printf(1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Exit status is that of
.I utility
if it ran; 127 if it could not be found on
.BR PATH ;
126 if it was found but could not be run; 1 on a usage error, a
too-large argument list or environment (internal caps: 48
environment entries, 32 argument words), or if the kernel itself
refuses the environment or argument list as too large; 0 if
.I env
only printed the environment.
.SH BUGS
A
.I name
given more than once among the
.IB name = value
operands is resolved by ORDER (the last one typed wins for any
program that looks it up), not by removing the earlier one --
a program that enumerates its own environment in full, rather
than looking up one name, may see both. No real shell ever
exports a duplicate name, so this has not mattered in practice.
"Found but could not be run" (exit 126) cannot be distinguished
here from other spawn failures below the two caps above -- this
machine's ProDOS has no separate execute-permission bit to test.
